# November 20, 2026: a first scatter across Alabama, the state with the fewest places on the map (one, Old Cahawba).
# Ground claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest (al). Spread from the Tennessee Valley to Mobile Bay: ironworks of the
# Civil War, territorial and colonial sites, Black Belt houses and a church, covered bridges.
# Sources read in this session: Wikipedia articles (tools/wiki_raw.py); the Alabama Historical Commission's "Plan your
# visit" and history pages (ahc.alabama.gov, read with tools/page.py); the park pages of Tannehill, Brierfield,
# Historic Blakeley, St. Stephens Historical Park and Sloss Furnaces (slossfurnaces.org);  the Encyclopedia of Alabama on Kymulga. Interiors were not visited by any source unless the entry says.
# Red brick: Belle Mont from Wikipedia ("built in red brick"); Sloss 'no'
# (photo: steel stoves and stacks); stone furnaces, wooden bridges and the board-and-batten church 'no' from their sources.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'al_hsqw','label':'Alabama: a first scatter, Tennessee Valley to Mobile Bay','added':'2026-11-20 03:23'}

def AHC(page,label):
    return ['Alabama Historical Commission: '+label,'https://ahc.alabama.gov/'+page]
NOPARK=' Parking was not checked on the ground.'

PLACES=[
N('tannehill-ironworks-al','Tannehill Ironworks','McCalla','al','industry','ruins',33.2477,-87.0679,'site','open','walk','burned','1830–1865',
  'Ironmaking here began with a bloomery forge in 1830. Moses Stroup built three charcoal blast furnaces between 1859 and 1862 that could make 22 tons of pig iron a day, most of it shipped to the Confederate naval gun works at Selma. Three companies of the 8th Iowa Cavalry burned the furnaces and foundry on March 31, 1865, during Wilson\'s Raid. A test here in 1862 showed that red ore could be used in Alabama furnaces, which helped bring ironworks to the Birmingham district.',
  'The furnace stacks, with reconstructed portions (Wikipedia calls it one of the best preserved 19th-century furnace sites in the South). The 1,500-acre park also holds the John Wesley Hall grist mill, the May Plantation cotton gin house and the Iron and Steel Museum of Alabama.',
  'Three Confederate furnaces burned by Union cavalry in the last weeks of the war.',
  'Tannehill Ironworks Historical State Park, run by the Alabama Historic Ironworks Commission; daily admission is charged (park page). The park page says parking lots, restrooms and trails are open every day.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Tannehill Ironworks'),['Tannehill Ironworks Historical State Park','https://www.tannehillstatepark.org']],brick='?',
  hours='Daily, sunrise to sunset, holidays included; visitor center 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('sloss-furnaces-birmingham','Sloss Furnaces','Birmingham','al','industry','standing',33.52066,-86.79131,'site','open','walk','','1882–1971',
  'Colonel James Withers Sloss began Birmingham\'s first blast furnace in 1881 on land given by the Elyton Land Company; it made pig iron from 1882 to 1971. The furnaces were rebuilt with modern equipment in 1927 to 1929. After closing it was given to the Alabama State Fair Authority, named a National Historic Landmark in 1981 and opened as a museum of industry in 1983: Wikipedia calls it the only blast furnace in the country preserved for public use.',
  'The furnaces, stoves, boilers and blowing machinery of a 20th-century ironworks, with signs through the site and a visitor center built in 2015. It also runs a metal arts program and holds concerts.',
  'The only blast furnace in the US kept whole for the public.',
  'A free self-guided tour; guided tours for a small fee Tuesday to Saturday at 10:30 a.m. and 2 p.m. (Sloss Furnaces visit page). The nearest mapped lots are on 32nd Street North and 28th Street North; whose they are was not checked.',
  [W('Sloss Furnaces'),['Sloss Furnaces: visit','https://www.slossfurnaces.org/visit']],brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday noon to 4 p.m.; free'),
N('brierfield-furnace-al','Brierfield Furnace (Bibb Naval Furnace)','Brierfield','al','industry','ruins',33.03944,-86.94889,'site','open','walk','burned','1861–1894',
  'Begun in 1861 by Caswell Campbell Huckabee and Jonathan Newton Smith with a 36-foot stone furnace and, in 1862, a rolling mill. The Confederate government forced its sale in 1863, named it the Bibb Naval Furnace and added a 40-foot brick furnace; the 10th Missouri Cavalry destroyed it on March 31, 1865. Rebuilt after the war, it closed for good in December 1894, and thousands of its bricks were carried off during the Second World War.',
  'The ruined brick furnace, the brick foundations of the rolling mill, the nailery foundations, coke ovens, the tramway bed and an 1850s cemetery, with a superintendent\'s house of about 1870. Log cabins, a store, a post office and an 1897 church have been moved into the park from nearby.',
  '',
  'Brierfield Ironworks Historical State Park (45 acres), run by the Alabama Historic Ironworks Commission; daily admission is charged (park page). The park warns that phone and internet service there are almost nil.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Brierfield Furnace'),['Brierfield Ironworks Historical State Park','https://brierfieldironworks.org/']],brick='?',
  hours='Daily, sunrise to sunset, holidays included'),
N('cornwall-furnace-cedar-bluff','Cornwall Furnace','Cedar Bluff','al','industry','ruins',34.24694,-85.58861,'site','public','walk','','1862–1874',
  'The Noble Brothers foundry of Rome, Georgia, built this furnace for the Confederacy from 1862, with an estimated 1,000 soldiers and enslaved men. It made pig iron for the foundry until Sherman\'s troops put it out of production in 1864, worked again from 1867 and shut in 1874. Cherokee County bought the furnace and 5 acres in 1975 and opened a park in 1977.',
  'A roughly pyramidal stack 45 feet tall, 30 feet square at the base, of large hematite blocks hauled 3 miles by ox cart from Dirt Cellar Mountain. A half-mile race from the Chattooga River ran through a tunnel under a hill to the water wheel; the bridge from the hillside to the stack top is gone. What is left of the race was not checked.',
  '',
  'A county park owned by the Cherokee County Commission (Wikipedia). No page of the county\'s for the park was found, so hours were not checked.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Cornwall Furnace (Cedar Bluff, Alabama)')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('janney-furnace-ohatchee','Janney Furnace','Ohatchee','al','industry','ruins',33.79472,-86.02056,'area','public','walk','burned','1863–1864',
  'Alfred Janney, a Montgomery businessman, built the furnace in 1863 after finding iron ore on the ground north of Ohatchee. A Union raid in July 1864 destroyed everything but the stone stack.',
  'A 50-foot stone furnace stack and the hand-dug reservoir that fed the steam engine for the blast. The park around it also holds a Confederate memorial of 2003 and a museum of 2009.',
  '',
  'Eli Henderson Park at Janney Furnace, a Calhoun County park (Wikipedia); the park\'s own site lists the museum and visitor center but gives no hours.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Janney Furnace Park'),['Janney Furnace Park','https://www.janneyfurnace.org/']],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('fort-toulouse-fort-jackson','Fort Toulouse and Fort Jackson','Wetumpka','al','fort','traces',32.50333,-86.25639,'site','open','walk','forts','1717–1814',
  'The French built Fort Toulouse in 1717 at the fork of the Coosa and Tallapoosa to hold the eastern edge of Louisiana, and rebuilt it about 1751. After the Battle of Horseshoe Bend in 1814 Andrew Jackson built Fort Jackson on the same ground. A 1970s replica was put up on the wrong outline, Fort Jackson\'s; in the 1980s it was taken down and a replica of Fort Toulouse built beside the original site. A National Historic Landmark since 1960.',
  'The 1751 Fort Toulouse replica and replica Creek houses, a partial recreation of the 1814 Fort Jackson earthworks, and a Mississippian mound of about 1100 AD (the Taskigi mound). The William Bartram nature trail runs through the park.',
  '',
  'A park of the Alabama Historical Commission, with an RV campground and a boat launch on the Tallapoosa; admission is charged (AHC visit page). The nearest mapped lot is on Jackson Park Road about 450 m south-east of the pin.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Fort Toulouse and Fort Jackson'),AHC('FtToulousePlanYourVisit.aspx','Fort Toulouse/Fort Jackson, plan your visit')],brick='?',
  hours='Grounds daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; visitor center Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('old-st-stephens-al','Old St. Stephens','St. Stephens','al','town','traces',31.55547,-88.03644,'site','public','walk','','1799–1829',
  'The territorial capital of Alabama, on a limestone bluff where rocky shoals stopped boats coming up the Tombigbee from Mobile. The park gives its heyday as 1799 to 1829; Wikipedia says George Gaines put up what may have been the first brick building raised by Americans in present Alabama there in 1811. Bypassed when the capital and the trade moved, its people resettled two miles inland at New St. Stephens on the railway, and the old town stood empty for over a century.',
  'An active archaeological site: work has mapped streets, building foundations, cellar pits and cisterns, and some digs are open to the public. The old town cemetery is beside it. The 1854 Washington County courthouse in New St. Stephens is now the visitor center and local museum.',
  'Alabama\'s first capital, left to the woods.',
  'St. Stephens Historical Park, a state park run to preserve the territorial capital, with a 70-acre quarry lake, 15 miles of trails and camping; tours of the town site by advance request (park page). Park hours and fees not checked.'+NOPARK,
  [W('St. Stephens, Alabama'),W('St. Stephens Courthouse'),['St. Stephens Historical Park','https://www.oldststephens.net/']],brick='?'),
N('blakeley-ghost-town-al','Blakeley and Fort Blakeley','Spanish Fort','al','town','traces',30.74222,-87.92417,'area','open','hike','','1813–1865',
  'Josiah Blakeley laid out the town in 1813 on the Mobile-Tensaw delta; its deep port rivalled Mobile, and it was the Baldwin County seat from 1820 to 1868. In April 1865 Union troops overran the Confederate works here in one of the last battles of the war, after building three rings of siege earthworks. Little of the town is left.',
  'Confederate breastworks that cross the park, on more than 20 miles of trails; the town site itself shows little. The park also offers battlefield tours and boat cruises into the delta.',
  'A rival to Mobile that vanished, and earthworks from one of the last battles of the Civil War.',
  'Historic Blakeley State Park, 34745 State Highway 225; admission $5 adults, $3 ages 6 to 12, free under 6 and for veterans; pets on leash (park page). Parts of the battlefield close for prescribed burns.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Blakeley, Alabama'),W('Historic Blakeley State Park'),W('Battle of Fort Blakeley'),['Historic Blakeley State Park','https://blakeleypark.com/']],brick='?',
  hours='Daily 8 a.m. to dusk'),
N('gaineswood-demopolis','Gaineswood','Demopolis','al','landmark','standing',32.50819,-87.83368,'site','open','drive','','1843–1861',
  'Nathan Bryan Whitfield, a cotton planter, designed and built the house himself over about 18 years, from a log dogtrot cabin in 1843 to its Greek Revival form in 1861; much of the work was done by enslaved people. By 1860 he held about 235 people in slavery. The Alabama Historical Commission runs it as a house museum.',
  'Stucco over brick scored to look like stone blocks, eighteen fluted Doric columns on the porches. Inside: domed ceilings with skylights in the library and dining room, a ballroom of fluted Corinthian columns and pilasters with facing mirrors, plasterwork throughout the main floor, and many original Whitfield furnishings. A cook\'s house, a Corinthian garden pavilion and a gatehouse survive.',
  '',
  'Alabama Historical Commission house museum; admission is charged; tour times by appointment, telephone 334-289-4846 (AHC visit page). Closed state holidays.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Gaineswood'),AHC('GaineswoodPlanYourVisit.aspx','Gaineswood, plan your visit')],brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Sunday by appointment only'),
N('st-andrews-church-prairieville','St. Andrew\'s Episcopal Church','Prairieville','al','worship','standing',32.51083,-87.7014,'area','view','drive','','1853',
  'The congregation was founded in 1834 and this, its first church, was built in 1853 by enslaved workers under enslaved master carpenters from the nearby Gallion plantation, who later founded the community of Freetown. Its design seems to come from a book by Richard Upjohn. Consecrated in 1858; the congregation declined after the war. A National Historic Landmark.',
  'A Carpenter Gothic church of vertical board siding with pointed-arch windows and buttress-like strips, on 3 acres on the south side of County Highway 12. Inside, Wikipedia says the walls are finished in wood with a tobacco stain. Interior otherwise not checked.',
  '',
  'Kept by the Episcopal Diocese of Alabama, with occasional services (Wikipedia); no service times found. Wikipedia says public access is allowed; seen from County Highway 12.'+NOPARK,
  [W('St. Andrew\'s Episcopal Church (Prairieville, Alabama)')],brick='no'),
N('belle-mont-tuscumbia','Belle Mont','Tuscumbia','al','landmark','standing',34.66199,-87.66709,'site','open','drive','','1828–1832',
  'A Jeffersonian plantation house built from 1828 to 1832 for Dr. Alexander W. Mitchell, an Edinburgh-trained doctor and slaveholder from Virginia; Isaac Winston bought it in 1833 and his family kept it until 1941. It was given to the Alabama Historical Commission with 33 acres in 1983 and is being restored in phases.',
  'Red brick: a raised two-storey centre with one-storey wings that run back in a U round a rear courtyard. Inside, a two-storey entrance salon between the dining room and parlour, furnished to before the Civil War with some Winston pieces. The outbuildings are gone; archaeologists found foundations, including a slave house.',
  '',
  'Alabama Historical Commission house museum; admission is charged; groups of 10 or more by appointment through the Colbert County Historical Landmarks Foundation, 256-381-5052 (AHC visit page). Closed state holidays.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Belle Mont'),AHC('BelleMontPlanYourVisit.aspx','Belle Mont, plan your visit')],brick='yes',
  hours='Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('mooresville-al','Mooresville','Mooresville','al','town','standing',34.62417,-86.88,'area','view','walk','','1818–',
  'Incorporated on November 16, 1818, while Alabama was still a territory, one of the oldest towns in the state, and home to 47 people in 2020. James Garfield, then a Union general, preached at its Church of Christ in 1863. The whole town is on the National Register.',
  'An early 19th-century village. The Stagecoach Inn and Tavern, the Brick Church, the post office and the Church of Christ are kept by the residents. Which open to visitors, and when, was not checked.',
  '',
  'A small lived-in town: look from the streets and respect the houses. The pin marks the town, not one building.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Mooresville, Alabama')],brick='?'),
N('horton-mill-covered-bridge','Horton Mill Covered Bridge','Oneonta','al','landmark','standing',34.00764,-86.44863,'exact','public','walk','','1934',
  'Built in 1934 over a deep gorge of the Calvert Prong of the Little Warrior River, replacing an 1894 bridge downstream named for Thurman Horton\'s mill. In 1970 it became the first covered bridge in the Southeast on the National Register. Damaged by vandals and closed in 2007, reopened in 2013, and closed to cars for good from April 1, 2022.',
  'A 220-foot Town lattice truss in two spans, about 70 feet above the water, which Wikipedia says is the highest of any covered bridge over a US waterway. Open to people on foot.',
  'The highest covered bridge in the country.',
  'Kept by the Alabama Historical Commission, Blount County and the state transportation department; walkers only. The lot OpenStreetMap maps about 70 m north fell on State Route 75 itself on the aerial, so parking was not checked.',
  [W('Horton Mill Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('kymulga-mill-covered-bridge','Kymulga Grist Mill and Covered Bridge','Childersburg','al','industry','standing',33.33389,-86.3,'site','open','walk','','1861–1977',
  'A four-storey grist mill built in 1864 for George Hoke Forney, run by three water turbines fed by a hand-dug canal and a wooden dam; the Encyclopedia of Alabama says it escaped the Union raids that burned so many mills, and it ground until 1977. The covered bridge beside it dates from 1861. Restored in 1974; the City of Childersburg has owned the park since 2011.',
  'The mill with its wooden dam and canal, and a 105-foot single-span Howe truss bridge of wood and iron over Talladega Creek, one of two 19th-century covered bridges in Alabama still where they were built; it leads to nature trails.',
  '',
  'Kymulga Park on Grist Mill Road (County Road 46), owned by the City of Childersburg and run by its historic preservation commission; admission is charged (Wikipedia; Encyclopedia of Alabama). The city\'s own page would not open, so hours were not checked.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Kymulga Mill & Covered Bridge'),['Encyclopedia of Alabama: Kymulga Grist Mill, Park, and Covered Bridge','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/kymulga-grist-mill-park-and-covered-bridge/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('clarkson-legg-covered-bridge','Clarkson–Legg Covered Bridge','Cullman','al','landmark','standing',34.20752,-86.99093,'exact','public','walk','','1904',
  'Built over Crooked Creek in 1904 and restored by the Cullman County Commission in 1975 with a grist mill and a log cabin beside it. A flood on Christmas Day 2015 closed the park until January 2016.',
  'A Town lattice truss over four spans, given as 270 feet (some sources say 250), the second longest historic covered bridge in Alabama; the grist mill and log cabin of the 1975 restoration stand in the park.',
  '',
  'Clarkson Covered Bridge Park on County Road 1043 off US 278, kept by Cullman County. The county parks page would not open, so hours were not checked. Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Clarkson–Legg Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('capitol-park-tuscaloosa','Old Capitol ruins, Capitol Park','Tuscaloosa','al','civic','ruins',33.2079,-87.5743,'site','public','walk','burned','1826–1923',
  'Alabama\'s capitol stood here from 1826 to 1846, designed by William Nichols. When the capital moved to Montgomery the building became Alabama Central Female College; it burned in 1923. By the Depression its ruins had been pushed into a low grassy mound; from 1988 a committee researched the site and built a ruin of it.',
  'Classical ruins that mix original ornament and 1980s rebuilding, and a marker on the college. The west end of the 4.5-mile Riverwalk starts here.',
  '',
  'A city park on Childress Hill above the Black Warrior River. Street parking and nearby lots; parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Capitol Park (Tuscaloosa, Alabama)')],brick='?'),
N('fort-mims-site','Fort Mims site','Tensaw','al','fort','marker',31.181,-87.838,'site','public','drive','forts','1813',
  'A stockade and blockhouse round Samuel Mims\'s homestead. On August 30, 1813, Red Stick Creeks under Peter McQueen and William Weatherford stormed it, killed almost everyone inside and took nearly 100 enslaved people captive.',
  'Nothing of the fort: reconstructed wooden walls and a blockhouse like those of the time at the south-west corner, markers and signs, the names of the families, and a recorded narrative at the picnic pavilion (AHC). A re-enactment is held the last weekend of August.',
  '',
  'An Alabama Historical Commission site (AHC visit page).'+NOPARK,
  [W('Fort Mims massacre'),AHC('FtMimsPlanYourVisit.aspx','Fort Mims, plan your visit')],brick='no',
  hours='Daily, daylight until dark'),
]

